=== LIGATURE CORRUPTION SCAN ===
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch01 - O'Brien - Being, Nothingness and Anxiety/bor-sec-45-obrien-anxiety-edges.csv:26:bor-sec-45,cites-cluster-author,"Dahlstrom, Daniel (2010) -- Negation and Being, The Review of Metaphysics 64","p.4 (n.5), p.26-27 (n.8)","Cited twice in O'Brien's footnotes for the treatment of negation/negative facts and the perception of movement/change as involving an ineliminable negative moment, underwriting O'Brien's own presence/absence argument (FAITHFUL-O'Brien, nn.5, 8, n.8 quoted at length). NOTE: Daniel Dahlstrom is also understood to be a contributor to this same edited volume (a later chapter per this cluster's sibling Hadjioannou-volume units, not yet built in this cluster at time of writing); this is an author-level cluster connection to a different work by the same person, not a citation of that volume chapter (P/anticipatory-application for the fellow-contributor framing; the citation itself is FAITHFUL-O'Brien)."
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch11 - Raffoul - The Ethics of Moods/bor-sec-54-raffoul-ethics-of-moods.json:66:    {"name": "Thomas Sheehan", "role": "'But What Comes Before the After?' (2018), cited for the claim that thrownness 'bespeaks' Befindlichkeit as the a priori fact of affective attunement to world; a different Sheehan publication from his own Ch.2 chapter in this same volume", "locus": "p.258"},
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Elpidorou - Boredom as a Concept in Phenomenology (2023)/bor-sec-06-elpidorou-concept-phenomenology.md:7:The entry's stated aim is to present three different phenomenological perspectives on boredom so as to show not just what boredom is, according to phenomenology, but what a phenomenology of boredom can reveal about human existence (p.1) (FAITHFUL-Elpidorou, p.1). It opens by staking out boredom's stakes for phenomenology generally: boredom gives rise to perceptions of meaninglessness, difficulties in effective agency, lapses of attention, an altered perception of the passage of time, and a diverse array of behavioral outcomes, and it demarcates the engaging and meaningful from what is not (p.1) (FE). Despite Heidegger's landmark 1929–30 treatment in *The Fundamental Concepts of Metaphysics* (FCM), the entry frames boredom as historically under-attended within phenomenology relative to how well-suited the method is to explicate it (p.1) (FE).
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Slaby - Living in the Moment (2017)/bor-sec-12-slaby-living-in-the-moment.md:24:- **monotony / meaninglessness** — central to the third form's "melody of duration has ceased" diagnosis (FAITHFUL-Slaby, p.241) and, in a different register, to Pessoa's "complete and absolute tedium" (FAITHFUL-Pessoa/Slaby, p.249).
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Slaby - Living in the Moment (2017)/bor-sec-12-slaby-living-in-the-moment.md:26:- **flow / engagement (contrast pole)** — present in two distinct registers: the dinner party's *outward* liveliness masking an underlying boredom (a surface/depth divergence, p.239–240), and, later, Pessoa's non-instrumental aesthetic absorption in writing as a wholly different, non-activist mode of "engagement" that never resolves into resolute action (FAITHFUL-Slaby, p.251–253).
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Seo et al - Machine Learning Approaches for Boredom Classification (2019)/bor-sec-31-seo.md:53:**Result.** Best model: k-NN, "1/distance" weighting, WSE-refined "10-2" feature subset, 1s window, 10-fold CV — **86.73% accuracy**, an 11.2-point gain over the unrefined 40-feature baseline (75.51%) (FAITHFUL-Seo et al., ~p.3842-3843). MLP and RF each peaked at 78.57% accuracy (AUC 0.858 and 0.866 respectively, feature group "10-6") (FAITHFUL-Seo et al., ~p.3842-3843). Lin et al. (2009) reported 92.57% for a different task using 30+ electrodes; Seo et al. read their own two-electrode result as a favorable usability/accuracy trade-off (FAITHFUL-Seo et al., ~p.3843).
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Elpidorou & Freeman - Affectivity in Heidegger I (2015)/bor-sec-02-elpidorou-freeman-affectivity-i.md:78:*(anticipatory-application)* This unit's chief service to Part III is infrastructural rather than substantive: it supplies the general theory of mood-as-disclosure — pre-reflective, world-constituting, prior to any subject/object split — that licenses treating student boredom, later in the dissertation, as something other than a private mental episode reportable only by introspection. The essay's insistence that mood is read off comportment and world-disclosure rather than off verbal report is a methodological caution Part III's own survey instrument should sit alongside, not substitute for: self-report captures an ontic trace of an attunement whose primary mode of givenness, on this account, is pre-reflective *(anticipatory-application)*. The essay's three-way disclosure structure (thrownness / world-as-a-whole / mattering) offers Part III a ready-made analytic grid for distinguishing *what* a bored VR or desktop-PC participant's boredom is disclosing — a determinate stimulus (thrownness-adjacent, first-form register) versus a diffuse loss of stakes in the whole task (second/third-form register) — prior to any physiological measurement *(anticipatory-application)*. The essay's own flagged Grundstimmung tension (anxiety in BT vs. profound boredom in FCM two years later) is a debate-axis worth carrying forward explicitly into the cluster's synthesis: Part III's poles (lab/VR vs. deployment/2D-PC) could be read as differentially eliciting situational (first-form-adjacent) versus diffuse (ground-mood-adjacent) boredom, and this unit is the earliest, clearest statement of why those two registers are not the same phenomenon merely differing in intensity *(anticipatory-application)*. Finally, the moods-ground-emotions proposal (§5, FE) is a caution against treating "boredom" and student "frustration" or "disengagement" (emotion-like, object-directed reports) as interchangeable in survey design: on this essay's own model they belong to different ontological registers, with boredom (a mood) the background condition for any more determinate affective complaint a student might voice *(anticipatory-application)*.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Darling - Synthesising Boredom (2023)/bor-sec-20-darling-edges.csv:37:bor-sec-20,shifts-debate-axis,unitary vs. plural boredom debate,"pp.3, 20","Predictive processing's resolution -- that emotion and mood are different hierarchical levels/timescales of one system -- is offered as dissolving rather than adjudicating whether boredom is 'one or many' (citing Elpidorou 2021; Tempelaar & Niculescu 2022) (FE)."
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/_synthesis/debate-map.json:75:   "resolution": "The cluster refuses the framing (P/anticipatory-application). A Grundstimmung is awakened, not ascertained (fcm-03); Hadjioannou independently makes the same argument-form for angst -- reflective access distorts the affect it targets (bor-sec-48). If so, self-report is not a noisy channel onto a hidden state but a different kind of act, and the dissociation is predicted rather than anomalous.",
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Review of Eye-Tracking Metrics (Preprint v3)/bor-sec-37-review-eye-tracking-metrics-edges.csv:26:bor-sec-37,cites-cluster-author,"Holmqvist, Kenneth, & Blignaut, P. (2020) -- Small eye movements cannot be reliably measured by video-based P-CR eye-trackers",~p.5,"Cited [46] on video-based eye-tracker measurement error; same lead author as this cluster's bor-sec-36 (Holmqvist et al., Eye Tracking: Empirical Foundations for a Minimal Reporting Guideline) -- a different paper, verified same-author match, not the identical work (FAITHFUL-Skaramagkas et al., ~p.5)."
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Review of Eye-Tracking Metrics (Preprint v3)/bor-sec-37-review-eye-tracking-metrics-edges.csv:27:bor-sec-37,cites-cluster-author,"Bolmont, M., Bianchi-Demicheli, F., Boisgontier, M.P., & Cheval, B. (2019) -- The Woman's Body (Not the Man's One) Is Used to Evaluate Sexual Desire",~p.6,"Cited [86], discussed in Section V-A1 re: automatic visual attention to sensual imagery; same author group (Cheval/Boisgontier) as this cluster's bor-sec-34 (Cheval et al., Physically Active Individuals Look for More) -- a different paper, verified same-author-group match, not the identical work (FAITHFUL-Skaramagkas et al., ~p.6)."
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Cheval et al - Physically Active Individuals Look for More/bor-sec-34-cheval.md:25:- **attentional disengagement / meta-awareness** *(P/anticipatory-application)* — the apparatus is built entirely around attentional orienting and engagement/disengagement, though never framed as boredom. The Introduction uses "disengage" analogously: a person "spontaneously allocate[s] attention toward physical activity opportunities... and... disengage[s] from opportunities to minimize effort" (FAITHFUL-Cheval et al., ~p.4). Read against this cluster's attention-failure boredom constructs (Eastwood et al.; Fisher/Hamilton), the gaze apparatus operationalizes the *same mechanism* — sustained vs. withdrawn visual engagement — applied to a different eliciting content (relevance/reward, not tedium). A structural, not textual, parallel.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Aroles & Kupers - Towards an Integral Pedagogy (2022)/bor-sec-15-aroles-kupers.json:45:      "summary": "This source does not engage FCM's boredom analysis at all. Its Heideggerian apparatus is drawn from two later, different texts -- 'The Question Concerning Technology' (1977, source of Gestell) and 'Discourse on Thinking' (1966, source of Gelassenheit) -- neither of which the authors relate to mood, Befindlichkeit, Stimmung, or boredom. Direct search of the full extraction confirms the words 'boredom,' 'mood,' and 'attunement' do not appear anywhere in the article. No bridges-to-fcm edge is warranted; forcing one would misrepresent the source.",
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Aroles & Kupers - Towards an Integral Pedagogy (2022)/bor-sec-15-aroles-kupers.json:85:    "Telepresence as loss vs. telepresence as a different (not lesser) embodiment: Challenge 1 stresses what 'gets lost' in disembodied telepresence (pp.761-764), while Challenge 2 insists 'cyberspace is not a disembodied reality' (p.765) and reframes the same phenomenon as a different kind of embodiment -- the article holds both framings without settling which is primary.",
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch04 - Hadjioannou - Angst as Evidence/bor-sec-48-hadjioannou-angst-evidence.md:31:FCM's claim (fcm-03, book p.62) is that treating a fundamental attunement as an object of "ascertaining" — of empirical or introspective demonstration — is not merely difficult but self-defeating: objectifying an attunement into an object of knowledge achieves the contrary of an awakening, since the attunement is thereby destroyed, or at least weakened and altered. Hadjioannou's chapter reconstructs, independently and from different textual material (BT and the 1923/24 IPR lectures rather than the 1929/30 FCM lectures), what is recognizably the same claim applied to angst specifically. Heidegger's most sustained argument for this, per Hadjioannou's reconstruction of §4.2.3, is that Husserlian "reflection" — the very act supposed to secure originary-intuitive evidence — is what distorts affective phenomena such as anxiety, joy, and terror (FAITHFUL-Hadjioannou, pp.87–88, citing IPR 220); Heidegger's own words, as quoted by Hadjioannou, insist that anxiety "is instead a phenomenon of existence itself" and cannot be grasped as a "manner of being-related-to-something" (direct quote ≤12 words, IPR 220, p.88). The same logic recurs at SZ 136, cited at p.81: dispositions disclose "prior to all cognition and volition" (direct quote, SZ 136, p.81), which is precisely why measuring their evidentiary status against the apodictic certainty of theoretical cognition is illegitimate (paraphrase, pp.81–82).
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch04 - Hadjioannou - Angst as Evidence/bor-sec-48-hadjioannou-angst-evidence.md:33:This is the same reflection-destroys-the-phenomenon structure FCM asserts for "ascertaining" an attunement — not a loose family resemblance but the identical inferential move (objectifying or reflectively grasping an affective/attunement phenomenon converts or destroys it, so evidence for it must come by a different, non-objectifying route) applied to a cognate but distinct target (angst rather than attunement-in-general or profound boredom). Because Heidegger works out recognizably the same argument at two different points in his career — 1923/24–1927 for angst-as-evidence, 1929/30 for attunement-in-general, culminating in profound boredom as the paradigm case — Hadjioannou's chapter can fairly be read as supplying the earlier, angst-specific instance of the very methodological move FCM later generalizes onto boredom (P/anticipatory-application: this synthesis across the two texts is ours, not either author's own claim).
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch04 - Hadjioannou - Angst as Evidence/bor-sec-48-hadjioannou-angst-evidence.md:39:A fourth, more incidental point of contact concerns the conscious/unconscious framing FCM explicitly refuses for attunement (fcm-03, §16b, book pp.60–62: waking/sleeping "are not equivalent to consciousness and unconsciousness"). Hadjioannou's own text, summarizing Berghofer's account of Conee and Feldman's mentalist evidentialism, notes in passing that "some unconscious, indeterminate state of anxiety may be a mental state" without thereby qualifying as a justifier (paraphrase ≤12 words, Berghofer 2018 as quoted at p.78–79). The two texts reach this point from opposite directions and for different reasons — FCM denies that conscious/unconscious is the relevant axis for attunement at all, while Hadjioannou's Husserl-derived framework merely denies that an unconscious mental state can do evidentiary work — but both converge on refusing to let the conscious/unconscious distinction settle the evidentiary or ontological status of an affective phenomenon. This is a weaker, more incidental parallel than the first two, and is recorded as such.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch04 - Hadjioannou - Angst as Evidence/bor-sec-48-hadjioannou-angst-evidence-edges.csv:33:bor-sec-48,bridges-to-fcm,fcm-03-awakening-18c,fcm-03 pp.60-62 (§16b) vs Hadjioannou pp.78-79 (Berghofer),"P/anticipatory-application (weak/incidental): both texts refuse to let conscious/unconscious settle the evidentiary or ontological status of an affective phenomenon, though for different reasons"
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch07 - Withy - Finding Oneself, Called/bor-sec-51-withy-finding-oneself.json:76:    "Angst as BT's sole Grundbefindlichkeit <-> boredom (from FCM) as an equally valid Grundbefindlichkeit for the call to being Dasein: Withy extends the ground-finding role, officially reserved for angst in BT, to profound boredom via FCM 143/165 (p.160), while marking the identification with a hedging '(?)' in her own Table 2 (p.169) and deferring full argument to Withy (2013). This corroborates, from a different route, the debate axis bor-sec-02 explicitly flagged and left open ('anxiety vs. profound boredom as candidate Grundstimmung').",
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch09 - Vallega-Neu - Truth, Errancy, and Bodily Dispositions/bor-sec-52-vallega-neu-truth-errancy.md:33:This chapter does **not** engage Heidegger's boredom analysis in *Fundamental Concepts of Metaphysics* (GA 29/30) in any substantive way. There is exactly one citation of the lecture course (GA 29/30: 238, p.206), offered solely as textual support for the general claim that grounding attunements — of which deep boredom is one example among several — are historically epoch-determining. Nothing is said about the three forms, the station-scene, *Zeitvertreib*, *Leergelassenheit*/*Hingehaltenheit*, the "telling refusal" (*Versagen*), or the *Augenblick* — the entire apparatus other units in this cluster (e.g., bor-sec-04, the Elpidorou and Freeman chapter that is chapter 8 of this same volume, immediately preceding this one) build their FCM bridges from. Nor does GA 29/30: 238 fall within the page range of FCM's own boredom chapters as cited elsewhere in this cluster (roughly FCM pp.78–168 in the English translation used for fcm-04 through fcm-07); Vallega-Neu's single citation is to a different point in the same lecture course, invoked generically rather than as engagement with the boredom material specifically. Accordingly, and per the instruction to check honestly rather than force a bridge, **this unit claims no fcm-0N bridge**. The single passing citation of "deep boredom" is real and is recorded (see Constructs, above, and the edges file), but it does not rise to the level of engagement the cluster's `bridges-to-fcm` relation is meant to capture.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch01 - O'Brien - Being, Nothingness and Anxiety/bor-sec-45-obrien-anxiety.md:33:The single point of textual contact, already flagged in §3 above, is the IM 1–2 passage in which Heidegger names boredom, alongside despair and joy, as one of three moods in which Leibniz's question "resounds" (FAITHFUL-O'Brien, p.11). This passage is itself worth situating precisely: it comes from *Introduction to Metaphysics* (the 1935 lecture course), which is a *different* text from *The Fundamental Concepts of Metaphysics* (the 1929/30 lecture course this cluster's fcm-04/05/06/07 units are built from), even though the two titles are easily confused. Heidegger's IM passage is a brief, unelaborated gesture — three moods named in one paragraph, with no train-station example, no "held in limbo," no "telling refusal," none of FCM's own apparatus. O'Brien cites it for its general illustration of how "bare moods" register something more than presence, not for any claim about boredom specifically, still less for FCM's tripartite structure.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch02 - Sheehan - Heidegger pathos as the Thing Itself/bor-sec-46-sheehan-pathos.md:27:This chapter does **not** engage Heidegger's boredom analysis in *Fundamental Concepts of Metaphysics* (GA 29/30) at all. There is no mention of *Langeweile*, the three forms of boredom, the station-scene, *Zeitvertreib*, or *Grundstimmung* anywhere in the text or its forty-one endnotes. Sheehan's argument operates one level up, at the general architecture of *Stimmung*/*Befindlichkeit* that FCM's boredom analysis presupposes and specifies: Sheehan explicitly notes that Heidegger's 1955 relocation of πάθος into *Stimmung* ("attunement to *Seyn*") repeats a move Heidegger "had previously done with *Befindlichkeit* (SZ §29)" (FAITHFUL-Sheehan, p.40). That is the *only* point of contact with the conceptual machinery FCM's three-form analysis puts to specific use on boredom; it is a general resonance (the same underlying doctrine of attunement-as-existential-structure), not a specific engagement with FCM's text or with boredom as a phenomenon. Accordingly, this unit claims **no fcm-0N bridge**: the honest and accurate statement is that Sheehan's chapter supplies further confirmation, from a different angle (a 1955 lecture rather than the 1929/30 lecture course), of the same general *Stimmung*-as-existential-structure doctrine that other units in this cluster (e.g., the Elpidorou & Freeman pieces) already connect directly to FCM's boredom chapters — but Sheehan himself draws no such connection, and it would be a fabrication to attribute one to him.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch11 - Raffoul - The Ethics of Moods/bor-sec-54-raffoul-ethics-of-moods-edges.csv:9:bor-sec-54,cites-cluster-author,bor-sec-46,p.258 (cross-cluster),"Thomas Sheehan is also the author of Ch.2 of this same volume/cluster (bor-sec-46, 'Heidegger: pathos as the Thing Itself'); Raffoul here cites a different 2018 Sheehan essay, not the Ch.2 chapter itself (P/anticipatory-application)"
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Raffaelli et al - The Knowns and Unknowns of Boredom (2018)/bor-sec-21-raffaelli.json:76:    "PRIMARY DEBATE-AXIS POSITION -- 'boredom as unified construct <-> heterogeneous family (Raffaelli review)' (this cluster's named axis, primarily grounded here): R,M&C do NOT resolve this axis to either pole. They diagnose the field's inconsistencies (chiefly around arousal and recruited brain regions) as compatible with TWO separable explanations -- (a) non-standardized operationalization of a single, unified construct across studies (a measurement-noise account that keeps boredom unified in principle), and (b) genuine construct heterogeneity, i.e. distinct boredom subtypes with different antecedents and signatures (a heterogeneous-family account). They treat (b) as a serious, evidence-backed hypothesis -- citing Goetz et al.'s (2014) five-category experience-sampling taxonomy as preliminary support (pp.2456-2457) -- and their own forward recommendation commits them to testing it: 'arriving at an empirically validated taxonomy of different types of boredom' is named as one of two necessary next steps (p.2451, abstract; p.2460). But they repeatedly hedge this claim ('preliminary evidence suggests...', 'if different types of boredom do indeed exist...', p.2451, p.2460), stopping short of asserting heterogeneity as established. Net position: an explicitly UNRESOLVED, dual-hypothesis diagnosis that treats heterogeneity as the more novel and forward-pointing (though unconfirmed) explanation, rather than a flat defense of construct unity.",
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Raffaelli et al - The Knowns and Unknowns of Boredom (2018)/bor-sec-21-raffaelli.json:79:    "Internal tension in the neural-correlates literature: hippocampus activation (Ulrich et al. 2014) vs. hippocampus deactivation (Mathiak et al. 2013) vs. no reported hippocampal involvement (Danckert and Merrifield 2016) under three different operational definitions of boredom -- offered by R,M&C as direct evidence that operationalization inconsistency, not just noisy measurement, may be driving apparently conflicting neuroimaging results (p.2458)."
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch12 - Slaby & Thonhauser - Affective (Un)Grounding of Politics/bor-sec-55-slaby-thonhauser-politics.json:80:    { "axis": "boredom's regulatory 'message' read as demanding individual authenticity (bor-sec-12/13's register) vs. read as demanding collective, Volk-level self-founding (this chapter's register)", "note": "Same FCM textual base, different addressee of the demand -- a genuine interpretive divergence across Slaby's own oeuvre, most visible when this unit is set beside bor-sec-12/13.", "locus": "p.278-280 (P/anticipatory-application for the cross-unit framing)" }
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hadjioannou (ed) - Heidegger on Affect (2019)/Ch12 - Slaby & Thonhauser - Affective (Un)Grounding of Politics/bor-sec-55-slaby-thonhauser-politics.md:13:The extraction supplied for this unit begins mid-sentence on book p.266 ("On the other hand, Heidegger's views on affectivity can figure as..."), missing the opening of an unlabeled introductory section that must begin on p.265 (the running headers — "12 Heidegger and the Affective (Un)Grounding of Politics" on recto pages, "J. Slaby and G. Thonhauser" on verso pages — confirm chapter identity and authorship on every subsequent page, so the missing material is only the introduction's first paragraph(s), not a different chapter). The extraction's end was verified directly: the chapter's own References list runs to book p.289–290 (ending with Withy 2015), immediately followed by the volume's cumulative back-of-book Index (beginning "Greek / ἀληθεύειν..."), which falls outside this chapter and outside this unit's scope. All analysis below is confined to book pp.266–290.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Sharma et al - Eye-Tracking and AI to Enhance Motivation/bor-sec-39-sharma.json:71:    { "axis": "AOI backtracking (this paper's construct) vs. literal text rereading (Millis & King 2001; Dowhower 1987)", "note": "the authors explicitly flag that their AOI-backtrack measure is conceptually different from the rereading construct in the cited literature, since students refer back to already-visible slide content rather than rereading previously completed material, cautioning that the rereading-comprehension literature's findings should be applied only cautiously to their own backtrack result.", "locus": "p.16" },
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Raffaelli et al - The Knowns and Unknowns of Boredom (2018)/bor-sec-21-raffaelli.md:7:Raffaelli, Mills, and Christoff (R,M&C) set out to synthesize, across psychology, neuroscience, and education, "the neural, cognitive, and behavioral correlates of boredom" (p.2451) into a two-column ledger: correlates that are "known" — consistently observed across studies — and correlates that remain "unknown" — inconsistently observed (p.2451, abstract). The paper is explicitly a roadmap rather than a new empirical study: its stated goal is "to provide a roadmap for researchers who wish to better understand boredom and further investigate boredom and its various correlates" (p.2451). Two framing choices matter for how this source should be used downstream. First, R,M&C restrict their scope throughout to boredom as a transient *state* rather than as a *trait* disposition (p.2452) — trait boredom (boredom proneness) appears only as an occasional secondary correlate, never as the review's target phenomenon. Second, the review's own diagnostic payoff, withheld until the Conclusion, is that the field's persistent inconsistencies (especially around arousal and the brain regions recruited during boredom) may trace to two distinct and separable causes: sloppy, non-standardized operationalization of what is treated as a single construct, and the live possibility — evidenced but not yet validated — that "boredom" may not name one construct at all but "an empirically validated taxonomy of different types of boredom" waiting to be characterized (p.2451, abstract; p.2460). The review does not adjudicate between these two explanations; it argues both need addressing before the field can make further progress.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Raffaelli et al - The Knowns and Unknowns of Boredom (2018)/bor-sec-21-raffaelli.md:29:- **Boredom-as-regulatory-signal (functional account)** is adopted, not merely reported: Bench and Lench's (2013) claim that boredom functions as "an emotional cue that one needs to pursue a goal different from what one is currently pursuing" (p.2452) is folded directly into the review's working definition of boredom, alongside Eastwood et al.'s (2012) aversive-state definition.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Raffaelli et al - The Knowns and Unknowns of Boredom (2018)/bor-sec-21-raffaelli.md:45:5. **The Conclusion's two-pronged diagnosis** (p.2460): R,M&C argue that the field's inconsistencies trace to (a) non-standardized operationalization of boredom across studies — different labs measuring different things under the same name — and, separately, (b) the open, unresolved possibility that distinct types of boredom exist with different antecedents and signatures. They recommend both a more precise, shared operational definition and dedicated empirical work to test — and, if warranted, formally validate — a taxonomy of boredom types, explicitly hedging the latter with "if different types of boredom do indeed exist" (p.2460).
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Raffaelli et al - The Knowns and Unknowns of Boredom (2018)/bor-sec-21-raffaelli.md:69:First, and most directly: this review is the primary in-cluster combatant on the axis *boredom as unified construct ↔ heterogeneous family*, and — like the Elpidorou and Freeman chapter's handling of the profound-boredom axis elsewhere in this cluster — it does not resolve cleanly to either pole. R,M&C's argued position is diagnostic rather than declarative: they show that the field's persistent inconsistencies (above all in arousal and in the recruited brain regions) are compatible with two distinct explanations, and they decline to pick between them. The first explanation keeps boredom unified in principle but indicts sloppy, non-standardized measurement across labs (p.2460). The second — supported by Goetz et al.'s (2014) five-category experience-sampling evidence and treated with genuine (if hedged) seriousness — holds that "boredom" may denote a family of related but distinct states with different valence-arousal signatures, which would make cross-study inconsistency a real empirical signal rather than mere noise (pp.2451, 2456–2457, 2460). Their own forward-looking recommendation — develop "an empirically validated taxonomy of different types of boredom" (p.2451, abstract) — commits them to taking the heterogeneous-family hypothesis seriously as an open, worth-testing possibility, while their repeated hedging ("preliminary evidence," "if different types of boredom do indeed exist," p.2451, p.2460) keeps them from asserting it as established. Part III should cite this review, not a generic "boredom research" gloss, whenever it needs a source that names the unified-vs-heterogeneous question as the field's central unresolved problem, rather than a source that argues for one side.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Raffaelli et al - The Knowns and Unknowns of Boredom (2018)/bor-sec-21-raffaelli.md:73:Third, the operationalization critique generalizes directly to survey design. Since R,M&C's own diagnosis is that much of the field's inconsistency stems from different studies measuring different things under the single label "boredom" (p.2460), Part III's own survey should be explicit about which operational definition its own items instantiate — e.g., an Eastwood-style "wanting, but being unable, to engage" item (p.2452) targets something conceptually different from a low-arousal-and-dissatisfaction item in the Mikulas-and-Vodanovich mold (p.2452), and the two need not covary.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hughes - Meaninglessness and Monotony in Pandemic Boredom (2023)/bor-sec-08-hughes-edges.csv:29:bor-sec-08,contests,relative significance of heterogeneous boredom experiences (READING/CLAIM),p.11-12,"Argues Heidegger's train-station/dinner-party examples cannot discriminate the differential political, social and economic significance of different people's pandemic boredom, forcing a fallback onto psychological state/trait-style categories (FAITHFUL-Hughes, p.11-12)."
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hughes - Meaninglessness and Monotony in Pandemic Boredom (2023)/bor-sec-08-hughes.md:16:4. **§4 "The significance of pandemic boredom"** (p.9–13, incl. conclusion) — argues pandemic boredom is essentially situative (first/second-level); argues it nonetheless realizes Heidegger's own *Contributions*-era worry that technologized indifference forecloses boredom's revelatory potential; then executes the paper's critical turn in two steps — the situative/existential distinction is destabilized by the pandemic's totalizing disruption of the everyday world itself, and the relative philosophical significance of different people's pandemic boredom cannot be discriminated without falling back on the heterogeneous social/political/economic conditions Heidegger's train-station and dinner-party examples were never built to address.
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hughes - Meaninglessness and Monotony in Pandemic Boredom (2023)/bor-sec-08-hughes.md:46:- Heidegger's account also cannot discriminate the relative philosophical significance of different people's pandemic boredom, because his train-station/dinner-party examples were never built to register the heterogeneous social, cultural, political and economic conditions that made the pandemic's harms unequally distributed; the survey data shows this bifurcation directly, with some respondents reporting renewed appreciation of relationships ("we seem to be getting along better," EN_UK_0046) and others reporting increased tension and distrust ("more arguments and destructive remarks," EN_UK_1074) under materially similar social-distancing measures (FAITHFUL-Hughes, p.11-12).
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hughes - Meaninglessness and Monotony in Pandemic Boredom (2023)/bor-sec-08-hughes.json:13:    { "title": "§4 The significance of pandemic boredom", "page": "9-13", "summary": "Argues pandemic boredom is essentially situative (first/second FCM level) and realizes Heidegger's own Contributions-era worry about technologized indifference foreclosing revelatory potential; then argues the situative/existential distinction is destabilized by the pandemic's totalizing disruption of the everyday world itself, and that the relative significance of different people's pandemic boredom cannot be discriminated without attending to heterogeneous social/political/economic conditions; concludes Heidegger's framework needs 'a more critical appropriation.'" }
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Hughes - Meaninglessness and Monotony in Pandemic Boredom (2023)/bor-sec-08-hughes.json:36:    { "claim": "Heidegger's train-station/dinner-party examples cannot discriminate the relative philosophical significance of different people's boredom given heterogeneous social, cultural, political and economic conditions; his account is forced back onto psychological state/trait and related dichotomies.", "type": "READING/CLAIM", "stance": "contests", "locus": "p.11-12", "tag": "FE" },
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Elpidorou - Boredom as a Concept in Phenomenology (2023)/bor-sec-06-elpidorou-concept-phenomenology.json:86:    "Unified vs. plural boredom -- the entry treats 'boredom' as genuinely three different phenomena under Heidegger (situational, retrospective-inauthentic, profound-ontological) while treating Husserl's and Sartre's constructions as accounts of a single, more unified (ordinary/everyday) boredom; it does not explicitly flag this asymmetry."
corpus/index/Boredom Secondary (Part III)/Murphy et al - Mundane Emotions (2023)/bor-sec-23-murphy.json:8:  "thesis": "Using Heidegger's (1995) FCM typology of superficial and profound boredom, the paper asks how different temporal dynamics structured consumers' experience of boredom during the COVID-19 pandemic. It argues superficial boredom (situational restlessness, desire for distraction) is produced by a dominant temporal regime of digital connectivity (fragmenting/de-sequencing time) and acceleration (compressing time), which together trap consumers in a recursive loop between boredom and digitally-mediated distraction; profound boredom (existential discomfort with one's sense of self) arises instead from an abundance of uninterrupted, solitary time -- exactly the condition pandemic lockdowns exceptionally produced -- and, following Heidegger, Benjamin and Gardiner, is read as having 'utopian,' self-transformative potential, catalyzing participants' discovery of new tribal passions. The paper concludes boredom is neither purely negative nor purely individual, closing with an account of what it means to be 'bored together.'",
(end)
